2026-02-10 01:54:54,190 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document The Rainbow Note

Rainbow notes offer exposure to multiple underlying assets in one instrument. For example, a single rainbow note might derive performance from three diversified assets like the Russell 3000 Index, the MSCI Pacific Ex-Japan Index (covering Asian markets outside of Japan), and a commodity index.

These notes often have lookback features, calculating returns based on the average value of the underlying assets over the note's term rather than just the final value at expiration. For instance, a quarterly lookback would average the index values after each quarter rather than using only the value at maturity. Option traders call this an "Asian option," distinguishing it from standard European or American options. This averaging mechanism often cuts down on the impact of short-term volatility, delivering more stable returns. is relevant
